      <journal id="j-fg-reason" title="Trust needs a ground" kicker="Why Gate Arch" unlockAfter="fg-reason" scoreFromHeldTier="true">
        <p>You already trust reason. You argue, count, and ask why. That trust is not a trick hanging in midair. Free-floating reason explains nothing; it still borrows the light it uses.</p>
        <p>John 1:1–9 names the Word as the true light. Romans 1:19–20 says what can be known of God is plain in what is made. Augustine’s illumination and Aquinas on intellect as measured by being both read that Light as the ground of our seeing — not a late accident at the end of a dark story.</p>
        <sources>
          <source>John 1:1–9</source>
          <source>Romans 1:19–20</source>
          <source>Augustine, De Magistro; Confessions VII</source>
          <source>Aquinas, ST I, q.16; q.84</source>
        </sources>
      </journal>
